<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Missile-raid gate profile. Mirrors gating.py:_attacker_predicates.

  This tree deliberately never routes to the policy. It used to carry the evader
  profile's ThreatClose / ThreatClosingFast branches as design intent; once an
  attacker policy existed to gate, the cumulative ablation (assault, 150 eps x 2
  blocks, scored as breach rate, higher is better for the attacker) came out
  monotonically downhill:

      scripted only .......................... 0.46 / 0.57
      + Committed -> scripted ................ 0.46 / 0.57   (no-op by design)
      + ThreatClose -> rl .................... 0.41 / 0.49
      + ThreatClosingFast -> rl .............. 0.37 / 0.38
      policy only ............................ 0.33 / 0.35

  So both RL branches were removed. What is left is one branch that routes to
  "scripted" and a default that does the same: kept because it states the run-in
  regime legibly in Groot2, not because it changes any behaviour.

  Contrast gate_defenders.xml, which gains 16 points from exactly one handoff.
  Same game, same machinery, opposite verdict: the defender's scripted law has a
  regime where it is structurally undefined (no positive intercept root, so it
  parks on a static gate point) and the missile's guidance law has no such
  regime — a committed run-in is precisely what that law is for.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="GateAttackers">
  <BehaviorTree ID="GateAttackers">
    <Fallback name="gate">
      <Sequence name="committed_to_scripted">
        <Committed/>
        <SetMode mode="scripted"/>
      </Sequence>
      <SetMode mode="scripted"/>
    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
